      "preview_head": "th Mozambique-South Tanzania. It is broadly acknowledged that natural resources conservation strategies affect the livelihoods of local communities. Moreover, evidence suggests that these livelihood i",
      "preview_tail": "cted not only shed light on the complexities surrounding community perceptions but could inform policymakers about best practices in aligning top-down regulatory frameworks with bottom-up participator",
      "full_text": "th Mozambique-South Tanzania. It is broadly acknowledged that natural resources conservation strategies affect the livelihoods of local communities. Moreover, evidence suggests that these livelihood impacts, in turn, can influence conservation achievements. Yet, what constitutes a conservation strategy that communities perceive as acceptable and thus they would be willing to commit to over time remains poorly understood. This study explores the perceptions of communities regarding the effects of two different conservation strategies in the Ruvuma landscape: governmental land concessions and licenses to private companies for resource extraction, versus community-managed areas where local people have more direct control or decision-making power. The research aims to identify how these differing approaches impact livelihoods through various dimensions such as economic benefits (e.g., employment opportunities), access to natural resources (such as water and firewood), social cohesion within the communities, cultural values preservation, security of land tenure, conflict levels with wildlife, among others. By understanding which aspects are most valued by local populations in each type of conservation strategy\u2014whether it\u2019s a sense of ownership over their environment or tangible economic gains\u2014the study seeks to contribute insights into designing more inclusive and effective community-based natural resource management programs. To gather this information, the research will employ qualitative methods including semi-structured interviews with key informants from various stakeholder groups (e.g., village leaders), focus group discussions within selected communities across both types of conservation areas. Additionally, quantitative data collection through household surveys might also be conducted to allow for a more robust analysis and comparison between different sites. The findings are expected not only shed light on the complexities surrounding community perceptions but could inform policymakers about best practices in aligning top-down regulatory frameworks with bottom-up participator",
